To force a text field to display columns in Adobe JavaScript, you can use the `text` property of the text field object to set the `multiline` property to `true` and adjust the `width` and `height` properties accordingly. Here's an example of how you can achieve this:

```javascript
// Get the text field object
var textField = this.getField("textFieldName");

// Set the multiline property to true
textField.multiline = true;

// Set the width and height of the text field
textField.width = 200; // Set the width in points
textField.height = 50; // Set the height in points

// Set the text alignment to left
textField.alignment = "left";

// Set the text to display in columns
textField.text = "Column 1 | Column 2 | Column 3";
```

In this example, replace "textFieldName" with the name of your text field. The `multiline` property is set to `true` to enable multi-line text input. The `width` and `height` properties are set to control the size of the text field. The `alignment` property is set to "left" to align the text to the left. Finally, the `text` property is set to the text you want to display in columns, with each column separated by a pipe symbol (`|`).

